# Classic Beans Usage Guide for Agents

This project uses **Classic Beans** for issue tracking. Issues are called beans. Use the `cbeans` CLI to manage them.

## Track All Work With Beans

User wants you to use beans for work tracking.

Typical workflow:
- First: Check if there already is an existing bean about this work. If there isn't, create a bean with `cbeans create "Title" -t <type> -d "Description..." -s in-progress`
- Then: Do the work, and keep the bean's todo items current (check off what has been done, as it happens; `- [ ]` → `- [x]`)
- Finally: Only if the bean has no unchecked todo items left, mark it completed with `cbeans update <bean-id> -s completed`.
- When comitting: Include both code changes AND bean file(s) in the commit

After finishing any task:

- When completing a bean, update it with a `## Summary of Changes` section describing what was done.
- When scrapping a bean, update it with a `## Reasons for Scrapping` section explaining why.
- Offer to create follow-up beans for any non-urgent work that was deferred.

## Finding Work

When the user asks what to work on next:

```bash
# Find beans ready to start (not blocked, excludes in-progress/completed/scrapped/draft)
cbeans list --ready

# View full details of specific beans (supports multiple IDs)
cbeans show <id> [id...]
```

## CLI Commands

```bash
# List beans
cbeans list                      # All beans
cbeans list --ready              # Beans ready to start (not blocked, excludes in-progress/completed/scrapped/draft)
cbeans list -t epic -s todo      # Filter by type and status
cbeans list -S "authentication"  # Full-text search

# View beans (supports multiple IDs)
cbeans show --json <id> [id...]

# Create a bean (always specify -t type)
cbeans create "Title" -t task -d "Description..." -s todo

# Update a bean (metadata, body, or both)
cbeans update <id> -s in-progress                        # Change status
cbeans update <id> --parent <other-id>                   # Set parent relationship
cbeans update <id> --blocked-by <other-id>               # Mark as blocked by another bean
cbeans update <id> --body-append "## Notes"              # Append to body
cbeans update <id> -s completed --body-replace-old "- [ ] Task 1" --body-replace-new "- [x] Task 1"  # Combine multiple options
```

Use `cbeans <command> --help` for full options.

## Relationships

- **Parent**: Hierarchy (milestone → epic → feature → task/bug). Set with `--parent <id>`.
- **Blocked-by**: Use `--blocked-by <id>` when THIS bean is blocked by another (this bean can't proceed until the other is done). **Prefer this when creating dependent work.**
- **Blocking**: Use `--blocking <id>` when THIS bean blocks another (the other bean can't proceed until this is done).
- **Implicit blocking**: A bean is also considered blocked if any of its ancestors (via parent chain) are blocked. Commands like `ready` respect this automatically.
- **Implicit status**: If a parent/ancestor has a terminal status (scrapped or completed), children inherit that status implicitly. `ready` excludes these beans.

